    "long_instructions": "In Lesson 9, this lab throws a runtime error, the code runs partway, then crashes, different from the last level's syntax error and the earlier song example's silent logic error. \r\n\r\nYou’re a QA engineer testing a program for a robot vacuum cleaner. The program keeps track of a number representing where it is. The program updates the number to move right or left, but there’s a bug.\r\n\r\n**Do This**\r\n\r\n1. Try to run the code!\r\n2. Read the error message. Use the error message to identify the bug in the program.\r\n3. Fix the bug and run the program again.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nIf you’re having trouble understanding an error message, you can copy the message into the AI Tutor to ask for help.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nReflection: Of the three bug types your students will see in this lesson, syntax, runtime, and logic, which do you expect will be hardest for them to debug, and why?\r\n\r\n::: details [**See a solution**]\r\n```\r\n# Use an int instead of a string\r\nposition = 5\r\n    \r\nprint(f\"📍 Currently at: {position}\")\r\n\r\n# Move right\r\nposition = position + 1\r\nprint(f\"📍 Currently at: {position}\")\r\n\r\n# Move right\r\nposition = position + 1\r\nprint(f\"📍 Currently at: {position}\")\r\n\r\n# Move left\r\nposition = position - 1\r\nprint(f\"📍 Currently at: {position}\")\r\n```\r\n:::\r\n",
